<p>&nbsp;From the summit, hikers can enjoy impressive views of the Almolonga, Quetzaltenango, and Llanos del Pinal valleys. The surrounding landscape also offers views of several volcanoes, including Santa María, Santiaguito, and Siete Orejas, along with other peaks in the region.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Approximately 1,200 years ago, Almolonga Volcano destroyed itself in a massive eruption. Today, the final section of the hike to Cerro Quemado follows steep, hilly terrain covered with rocks, making the ascent more challenging. The area is considered sacred land, and hikers may have the opportunity to witness traditional Mayan ceremonies during their visit. On the return journey, visitors can also relax in natural hot steam baths available at several locations along the way.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The hike is available seven days a week, with departures usually scheduled in the morning or early afternoon. A 7:00 a.m. departure is offered at a minimum of two participants. The cost is Q150.00 per person, approximately US$20.00. Tips are not included in the price.</p>
<p>&nbsp;All profits from the hike are donated to the La Pedrera Community Project, a non-profit organization that supports more than 60 children by providing after-school academic assistance. For more information about the project, visit www.lapedreraschoolproject.com.</p>
